# PROSPECTIVE summary scaffold, generated by scripts/new-provenance-summary.sh.
# TODO: replace the placeholder summaryText/decisionPoint below with the
# actual distilled reasoning behind this PR before committing — see
# docs/plans/TRANSCRIPT_SUMMARY_GUIDELINES.md. Timestamps below are real
# (from `git log main..HEAD` on branch feat/526-write-route-concurrency-limit); everything else that
# isn't marked TODO can be left as-is.

ghpull:548 a bl:PullRequest, bl:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:label "sparql_endpoint: concurrency limit for write routes" ;
    bl:number 548 ;
    bl:state bl:Open ;
    bl:closesIssue ghissues:526 .

session:claudeSonnet5 a prov:SoftwareAgent ;
    rdfs:label "Claude Sonnet 5" .

session:pr548 a agp:AgentSession ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith session:claudeSonnet5 ;
    prov:used ghissues:526 ;
    prov:startedAtTime "2026-08-13T22:23:27+02:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-08-18T02:44:01Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

session:pr548Summary a agp:TranscriptSummary ;
    agp:summaryText "Issue #526 asked for a judgment call, not a mandated implementation: decide whether a write-route concurrency limiter is worth adding given the datastore's RwLock already serializes writes, so a limiter cannot help lock contention. Reading the write handlers first showed the RwLock argument was correct but incomplete: all six write routes fully buffer/parse their body -- up to 64 MiB each by default -- before ever reaching the lock, so N concurrent large uploads multiply peak in-flight memory by N regardless of lock serialization. That concrete harm mechanism justified implementing the limiter rather than skipping it. Chose tower::limit::GlobalConcurrencyLimitLayer over ConcurrencyLimitLayer after reading tower 0.5.3's source: ConcurrencyLimitLayer::layer() mints a fresh semaphore per call, so applying it per route would give six independent budgets rather than one combined total; GlobalConcurrencyLimitLayer's shared Arc<Semaphore> matches the real need (N in-flight writes summed across all six routes). Wired Config::max_concurrent_writes and --max-concurrent-writes/DAGALOG_MAX_CONCURRENT_WRITES following the --request-timeout pattern from #367/#423, default 64 (bounds worst case to about 4 GiB). Hit an axum type-inference wall chaining two .layer() calls (body-limit, concurrency-limit) on one MethodRouter -- NewError couldn't be pinned across two hops -- fixed by combining both into one tower::ServiceBuilder stack applied as a single .layer() call, concurrency-limit outermost. #367's deferral of this exact feature flagged 'the (N+1)th write queues' as flakiness-prone to test, and this session had just hit that class first-hand on #527/PR #543 (a debug-sized margin that broke under release). Applied that lesson: the queuing test withholds connection A's last body byte under the test's own write_all call, not a timer, and asserts connection B gets zero bytes while A is held -- only a false negative is possible, never a flaky false positive. The one fixed sleep (200ms, before connecting B) bounds OS/tokio scheduling latency, not CPU-bound work, but cargo test --release was still run explicitly and passed (3/3, 0.72s). A stray already-committed temp file was found and removed as incidental cleanup, called out in the PR body." ;
    agp:reasoningFor ghpull:548 ;
    agp:decisionPoint session:pr548Decision ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy session:pr548 ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo session:claudeSonnet5 .

session:pr548Decision a agp:Decision ;
    agp:summaryText "Implemented the write-route concurrency limit rather than leaving it unimplemented/effectively-unbounded, because a concrete harm mechanism was found: every write route buffers its whole body in memory before reaching the RwLock, so unbounded concurrent large uploads multiply peak memory even though the lock serializes the actual writes. Used tower::limit::GlobalConcurrencyLimitLayer (one shared Arc<Semaphore> across all six write routes) rather than ConcurrencyLimitLayer (which would silently give each route its own independent limit), applied via one combined tower::ServiceBuilder stack per route to sidestep an axum MethodRouter::layer type-inference failure from chaining two separate .layer() calls." ;
    agp:alternative "Considered implementing only a token/effectively-unbounded default (per the issue's own fallback suggestion) if no concrete harm mechanism beyond 'defense in depth' could be found. Rejected once the body-buffered-before-lock memory-multiplication mechanism was confirmed by reading the actual handler code, since that is a real bound worth having, not speculative hardening." .
